.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:474:    93	Providers may invoke external systems and return `Completion`; they must not write DB rows, `intelligence.json`, signals, claims, or UI events directly. Mutations stay in services and existing queue write paths, preserving the services-only rule. Intelligence Loop 5-question check: no schema, signal type, health-scoring rule, briefing surface, or feedback hook is added here.
.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:505:12:DailyOS has two composers that produce scores from entity signals: the **Trust Compiler** ([DOS-5](https://linear.app/a8c/issue/DOS-5)) which scores per-claim trustworthiness for the provenance envelope, and the **Account Health Composer** ([ADR-0097](0097-account-health-scoring-architecture.md)) which scores per-dimension health for briefings. As drafted, both reimplement the same primitive calculations — source reliability from Bayesian posteriors, freshness decay, corroboration boosts, contradiction penalties, user-feedback modulation. The math is identical; only the composition differs (weighted geometric mean for trust, lifecycle-weighted arithmetic for health).
.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:569:14:1. [ADR-0102](0102-abilities-as-runtime-contract.md) makes every capability a typed, versioned ability. Many of those are Transform abilities that produce claims. The product will ship with multiple ability authors — `prepare_meeting`, `detect_risk_shift`, `generate_weekly_narrative`, future MCP agents — each asserting claims into the same substrate.
.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:572:18:Underneath these three is a harder question the substrate as drafted does not answer: **who is allowed to commit a claim, and when does a claim become load-bearing versus merely under consideration?** Today the answer is implicit: whoever writes it. That works when DailyOS is one engineer's enrichment pipeline. It stops working the moment an LLM-backed agent tries to assert "the champion is departing" based on a faint signal, and the briefing tomorrow morning treats that as ground truth.
.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:578:35:| `agent:<name>:<version>` | `agent:prepare_meeting:1.3` | `agent:detect_risk_shift:2.1` | LLM-backed or mixed-mode DailyOS ability producing claims |
.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:598:96:- **Transform abilities** produce `AbilityOutput<ClaimProposal>` — synthesized assertions with full provenance, but no side effect on `intelligence_claims`.
.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:624:152:Version bumps reset the ledger: `agent:prepare_meeting:1.3` and `agent:prepare_meeting:1.4` are different actors. Rationale: a prompt or logic change can materially change behavior; we don't inherit credit or blame across versions.
.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:628:162:e.g., `agent:prepare_meeting:1.3-p2.1`. A prompt edit bumps `prompt_template_version`, which changes the composite ledger key, which triggers trust warming on the new key per R1.4. This preserves the intent that prompt changes reset trust without abusing `ability_version` semantics.
.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:645:209:[ADR-0107](0107-source-taxonomy-alignment.md)'s `DataSource` enum is **not** extended with new variants for actors. `DataSource` describes the *origin of information* (Glean downstream, Clay, Gong transcript, user input). `actor` describes *who asserted the claim*. A single agent claim has `actor = 'agent:prepare_meeting:1.3'` and sources `[Glean { downstream: Salesforce }, Glean { downstream: Gong }]`. The two axes are orthogonal and remain so.
.docs/plans/wave-W4/_prompts/_logs/DOS-5.log:656:237:- **Propose/commit makes trust operational.** Below-threshold agent output is isolated in the Analysis Inbox rather than leaking into briefings.
